00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.020
And let's get back to the story now.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.400
Clarence Saunders, what a man.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.160
- So my great grandfather's
grocery store in rural Tennessee

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.300
was like almost every
grocery store in America.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.970
It was called a full
service grocery store.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.350
You walked in, there was a counter,

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:45.580
you often handed the grocer list,

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.250
and then the grocer would
go and get all of your food.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:51.810
And then they would weigh it
all, and they would charge you,

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:56.370
and often, you would pay
back that grocery bill

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:57.890
over time on credit.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:59.380
There were no aisles.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.740
Customers usually didn't pick brand names,

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.940
so brand named food wasn't
nearly as important as it is now.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.550
And also, highly processed packaged food

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.220
wasn't nearly as important as it is now.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:15.080
- The first thing this
guy, Clarence Saunders,

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:16.810
did was he had an idea.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.060
- He would later like,
tell the origin story

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:22.330
of many different ways, but
one of the ways he told it,

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:25.200
he would say, "I was on a train,

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.520
and like a bolt of lightning,

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:32.090
the idea hit me for the
future of American food."

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.640
- [Announcer] A daily battle
is being waged in supermarkets

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.200
all over this country.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.253
A battle for the customer's dollar.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:42.370
- His concept for a grocery
store was a store with aisles

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:45.210
where customers themselves
would walk through the aisles

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:46.750
and pick their own food.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:48.730
There would be price tags on the food,

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:51.350
which had never been the case before.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:55.780
And he would call this
grocery store Piggly Wiggly.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:57.150
- Wait, what?

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:58.380
Piggly Wiggly?

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.440
Piggly Wiggly, why?

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:01.273
Wait, why?

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:04.320
- That's a great question, Johnny.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.270
So one time, Saunders was asked about this

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.407
and he answered that the name
of the grocery store, quote,

00:05:10.407 --> 00:05:13.800
"arrived from out of chaos
and in direct contact

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.750
with an individual's mind."

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.670
Which maybe gives you a
little bit of a concept

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:19.860
of the sort of dude her was.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:23.181
- Some men just want to
watch the world burn.

00:05:23.181 --> 00:05:24.220
(Johnny laughing)

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:26.560
- Oh, that is amazing.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.147
- But mostly, when people would ask,

00:05:28.147 --> 00:05:31.470
"why call your grocery
store Piggly Wiggly,"

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.920
he would answer, "so that people will ask

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.950
that very question."

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:39.910
- Man, this feels like
the Old Spice commercials.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:41.699
- [Woman] Pair Charmin
Freshmates with your Char-

00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:45.050
- (yells) Old Spice body
spray is too powerful

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:46.550
to stay in its own commercial!

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.190
- Yes.
- We're going to just be like,

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:53.160
so weird and crazy that like,
everyone will be curious.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:55.610
- And a huge percentage
of his advertising budget

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:58.650
went to ads that were so weird.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:03.460
I mean, he called the third
Piggly Wiggly Piggly Wiggly III

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:08.130
because he felt that it was
owed a certain royal dignity.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:12.450
I mean, it was very much
like the early 1920s and 30s

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.057
version of the Old Spice ads.

00:06:15.057 --> 00:06:16.190
(Terry Crews yelling)

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:19.240
- Okay, so Clarence Saunders
basically just disrupted

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.000
the old way of grocery shopping.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.400
And as a consequence, a bunch of clerks,

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.590
these people who used to
do the grocery shopping,

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:26.860
lost their job.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.230
- My great grandfather's
grocery store closed

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:31.660
as did many, many others.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:33.450
A lot of jobs were lost in this.

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.320
And there was widespread
fear about, you know,

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:39.830
losing jobs to automation,
losing jobs to hyper-efficiency,

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:43.300
which also, of course,
foreshadows some current concerns.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:46.010
- Technology today could lead to 45%

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:47.700
of current jobs disappearing.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.160
- But that doesn't make him an asshole.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:51.410
Like, there are people all
the time making businesses

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:53.920
that people lose jobs on and
we sort of freak out about it,

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:55.290
but it's actually better for the world.

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.660
Oh, and he was pioneering
new ways of like,

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:00.450
guerrilla marketing that made
people talk about him more,

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:01.930
which is a really cool thing, too.

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:04.200
It seems like this guy changed
the world for the better.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:05.040
And he did.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:08.463
But he also starts to give
us signs of who he really is.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.080
- So even early on, there
were some indications

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:16.380
that Saunders was a bully,

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:19.570
that he could be very cruel to employees,

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:22.350
that he could shortchange partners.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.560
And he was obsessed with growth,

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:27.320
he was obsessed with making Piggly Wiggly

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:29.810
as big as possible as fast as possible,

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:31.870
taking over the entire world.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.510
In fact, even when Piggly Wiggly was a

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:37.570
Memphis-only phenomenon
in the very early days,

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:39.927
he would put on the
windows outside the store,

00:07:39.927 --> 00:07:42.890
"Piggly Wiggly: All Over The World."

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:44.090
- So this gets to my question.

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:46.670
As someone who is building
a startup right now

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:49.380
and thinking a lot about
building a company,

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:51.300
I genuinely have this question.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:55.340
Do you really need to be the
mean bully founder and boss

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.710
to make a world-changing business?

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:59.610
We'll try to answer that
question in a little bit,

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.720
but first, I got to tell you the next part

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.300
of the Clarence Saunders story.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.373
Because it's just too good not to tell.

00:08:05.373 --> 00:08:09.460
(soft percussion music)

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:11.400
- This is where we start to see

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:14.730
a lot of the negatives
of Clarence Saunders.

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:17.740
He became obsessed with trying to

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:20.690
prove these Wall Street speculators wrong.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.650
And as he lost control of the business,

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:27.240
he became more and more
angry and vitriolic

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:29.470
and really hateful.

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:31.930
He wrote about this, at
times, quite beautifully.

00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:35.590
Like, once he wrote, "they have
it all, everything I built,

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:38.980
the greatest stores of
their kind in the world,

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:42.640
but they didn't get the man
that was father to the idea.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.980
They have the body of Piggly Wiggly,

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:46.940
but they didn't get the soul."

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:48.030
And I just love that.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:51.910
I mean, how much do you
have to believe in yourself

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:54.750
to be like, "I am the
soul of this company."

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:57.090
So his next idea isn't quite the same

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:58.410
as the original Piggly Wiggly.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:02.190
It's actually much closer to
contemporary grocery stores

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:04.400
because there are still aisles

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:06.160
where people pick out a
lot of their own food,

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:09.440
but there are also staffed counters

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:12.520
for the deli department
and for the bakery.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:14.010
As he's developing this new idea,

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.890
he wants to call the
grocery store Saunders.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:18.920
Because of course he does.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.617
But the people who own
Piggly Wiggly are like,

00:09:21.617 --> 00:09:22.590
"no, no, no, no, no."

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:26.110
You are not allowed to use your name

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:28.270
in selling a grocery store

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:31.430
because that would be a direct
competitor to Piggly Wiggly,

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:34.760
and that is a violation of our agreements.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:38.060
And in response, Clarence Saunders decides

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.360
to call his new grocery store

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:45.180
the Clarence Saunders Sole
Owner Of My Name grocery store.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:47.220
- Oh my gosh, it's so good.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.750
It's like, I didn't
realize you could one-up

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:52.070
this sort of self-centeredness and like,

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:54.840
snarkiness of this guy.
- Yes, yes.

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:57.680
- And then it's like, again,

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:59.480
he's doing this to his business.

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:01.980
Like, this is what is going
to be out there to the world,

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:06.810
and yet he's using it as
his like, personal ego-fest.

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:07.860
I love it.

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:09.700
- But that's a lot of what we see

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:11.860
with 21st century capitalism too,

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:16.620
is these highly charismatic
individual founders

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:21.190
who are really lifted up as
the centerpiece of the company,

00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:25.570
that it's not about the
75,000 or 200,000 employees

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:27.640
working together on a project.

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:31.080
It's about this one god man, you know?

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:32.840
Almost always a man.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:36.210
- My question is now for you as a person

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:40.520
who's grown businesses and has led people,

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:42.180
and I'm in this now more and more

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:44.580
as I grow like, a startup and stuff.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:47.460
And like, Clarence Saunders
was obviously very successful.

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:48.760
Like, timing might've
been a little bit off,

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:50.620
but like, he was very
successful in disrupting

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:52.130
and changing things
and building a business

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:53.420
that still exists today.

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:55.940
Do you have to be an asshole to like,

00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:59.320
build big businesses in
the capitalistic system?

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:01.710
Like, is that a prerequisite to like,

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:04.570
be hyper successful, in your mind?

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:05.720
- I'm not sure.

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:10.420
I was on a phone call once
with a very successful

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:12.840
entrepreneur person.

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:17.840
And he said to me, "you're
too loyal to your people

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:21.570
and not loyal enough to your product."

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.160
And my feeling about that was well,

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:25.710
if that's the side that
we're going to err on,

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:28.130
that's the side I would prefer to err on.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:32.070
Like, I believe in humans
making stuff together.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:37.070
I don't really believe in the
idea of these sui generis,

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:40.870
brilliant individuals who
reshape history on their own.

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:45.870
I mean, the truth is, like
almost every successful person,

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:50.430
Clarence Saunders was a
bubble on the tide of empire,

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:51.710
as Robert Penn Warren put it.

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:56.710
He was riding a wave that was
much, much larger than him.

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:01.830
And, I mean, he was a very bright guy,

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:04.340
but I think that in the end,

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:07.230
that obsession with growth at all costs,

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:12.110
that obsession with, you
know, winning, it ruined him.

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:16.300
I mean, he died a poor man in a

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:18.770
sanitarium for mentally ill people.

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:23.770
He alienated almost everyone
who loved him in his life.

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:28.276
And I don't think that's
the hero's journey.

00:12:28.276 --> 00:12:30.693
(calm music)

